      ":question/problem": "Consider the infinite list of sequences with elements from $A = {1, 2, 3}$, as shown below.\n$Sequence 1:\\begin{vmatrix}a_{11}, & a_{12}, & \u2026, & a_{1n}, & \u2026 \\\\ Sequence 2: & a_{21}, & a_{22}, & \u2026, & a_{2n},\\end{vmatrix}\u2026; \u22ee\\begin{vmatrix}\u22ee & \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ Sequencen: & a_{n1}, & a_{n2}, & \u2026,\\end{vmatrix}a_{nn},\\begin{vmatrix}\u2026 \\\\ \u22ee\\end{vmatrix}\u22ee\\begin{vmatrix}\u22ee & \u22ee\\end{vmatrix}\u22f1$\nTo construct a new sequence that is guaranteed to be different from each of the given sequences on the list, we can use Cantor's diagonal argument, as follows:\n\nDefine the new sequence as\n$b_{1}, b_{2}, \u2026, b_{n}, \u2026$\nwhere $b_{i} \u2208 {1, 2}$ for $i \u2208 N$, and\n$2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"
